  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "India to keep Russian oil imports near current levels after Trump waiver",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-to-keep-russian-oil-imports-near-current-levels-after-trump-waiver-126101700454_1.html",
      "description": "India is expected to keep importing Russian crude at close to current levels after US President Donald Trump waived sanctions and tariffs mandated by a new law on major buyers of Russian oil, analysts said.\nTrump issued an executive order late on Friday waiving the \"sanctions, restrictions, and duties\" under the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act, a day before a 30-day deadline for enforcement expired. He signed the law on September 18.\nThe law authorised tariffs of up to 100 per cent on goods from the five largest importers of Russian crude and gas, and sanctions on Russia's shadow fleet of tankers. The waiver is not specific to India and could also benefit China. The order lets the administration reconsider it later.\nIndia is the largest importer of Russian seaborne crude, averaging 1.8 million barrels per day this year, ahead of China at 1.2 million, ship tracking data showed. Italy, Turkey and the Netherlands completed the top five.\nIndia took about 1.82 million",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-17T09:12:18"
    },
    {
      "title": "Trump's sanctions waiver offers India temporary relief, no certainty: GTRI",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/trump-s-sanctions-waiver-offers-india-temporary-relief-no-certainty-gtri-126101700282_1.html",
      "description": "US President Donald Trump's waiver from sanctions, restrictions and tariffs under the Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act (SRIA) gives India temporary relief but offers no lasting certainty, and New Delhi should safeguard its energy security and buy oil on commercial terms, think tank GTRI said on Saturday.\nIt said that the move gives India relief from threatened additional duties of up to 100 per cent linked to Russian oil purchases.\n\"But the relief is reversible. Washington can restore the tariffs anytime, leaving India and other buyers uncertain about future US action,\" the Global Trade Research Initiative (GTRI) said.\nIt also said SRIA remains a law, leaving Washington free to revive the tariff threat to extract concessions in bilateral trade talks.\nThe think tank added that Russia's share of Indian oil imports fell from 51 per cent in July to 44 per cent in August as India bought more from the US, despite higher prices, and from the UAE.\n\"For India, the waiver buys time but offer",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-17T06:01:59"
    },
    {
      "title": "Trump waives Russia energy tariffs and sanctions under Graham law",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/trump-waives-russia-energy-tariffs-and-sanctions-under-graham-law-126101700093_1.html",
      "description": "Waiving the law avoids a potential clash with the top purchasers of Russian energy products, India, China and Turkey",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-17T01:55:43"
    },
    {
      "title": "Trump waives broad new Russia sanctions and tariffs under Graham Act, citing national interest",
